   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.9",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Multiple Representations of Limits",
    "stem": "A graph of f is described as follows: as x approaches 2 from either side, the y-coordinates approach 5. The plotted point at x = 2 is (2, 9). Which statement in limit notation matches the graph’s approach behavior?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "$\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=9$",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Function-value substitution: this uses f(2) instead of the value approached nearby. The plotted point gives f(2) = 9, but the surrounding y-coordinates approach 5. Fix: Track nearby y-values rather than the point at x = 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "$\\lim_{x\\to 5} f(x)=2$",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Input-output reversal: this exchanges the approaching x-value with the approached function value. The description says x approaches 2, not 5. Fix: Place the approaching x-value under the limit and the approached y-value after the equals sign."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "$\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ does not exist.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Point-limit confusion: this treats the different value at x = 2 as preventing a limit. Both sides approach 5, so the limit exists regardless of f(2). Fix: Compare the approached values from both sides before deciding whether the limit exists."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "$\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=5$",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The x-coordinates approach 2 while the function values approach 5 from both sides. Limit notation records this approach behavior, not the value at the plotted point."
     }
    ]
   },
